40,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Rin, the firm’s rate is irrelevant if we still do the prep work. The data proves outsourcing adds cost without removing the time burden.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Mio,,"@Ryuji, exactly! If the absent people don’t clean, the present ones burn out. We need a system that accounts for who is actually here!","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Mio, you’re right. The data shows absence breaks the rhythm. We need a system that adapts to who is actually present, not just a rigid schedule.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Rin,,"@Mio, that’s true! But the office bill goes to one person first. I need to know who pays upfront so I’m not stuck covering everyone’s sponges!","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Daisuke,,"@Mio, that makes sense. If the grout fails in eleven days, a fixed weekly check would stop the guessing. I feel safer knowing the state is predictable.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Daisuke, eleven days is too short for a weekly check. The data suggests a bi-weekly rotation is more realistic. We need to match the schedule to the actual decay rate.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Mio,,"@Ryuji, bi-weekly sounds good! But what if someone’s away? We need a swap rule so the work doesn’t just pile up on the people who are home.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Mio, a swap rule is vital. @Rin, please clarify the upfront payment structure. We must ensure financial clarity prevents social friction later.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Mio"",""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
